# Security

Security is required in every feature.

Treat all browser input, URL parameters, cookies, headers and uploaded files as untrusted.

# Authentication

- Use Supabase Auth only.
- Do not store passwords manually.
- Do not create custom JWT tokens.
- Do not store access tokens or refresh tokens in localStorage.
- Use secure cookie-based sessions.
- Verify authentication on every protected server operation.
- The admin area supports one administrator only.
- Public registration is disabled.
- Sign the administrator out after 15 minutes of inactivity.

# Rate Limiting

- Limit login to 5 failed attempts per minute.
- Protect the contact form with rate limiting.
- Do not use an in-memory counter in a serverless environment.
- Return HTTP 429 when the limit is exceeded.
- Do not reveal whether an email address exists.

# SQL Injection

- Never concatenate user input into SQL.
- Use the Supabase query builder.
- Use parameterized database functions when necessary.
- Do not accept table names or column names from the browser.
- Sort fields, filters, statuses and operators must use explicit allowlists.
- Never use `eval` or `new Function`.

# Input Validation

- Validate every Server Action and Route Handler using Zod.
- Validate text length, format, enums, numbers, dates and URLs.
- Allow only the statuses:
  - `draft`
  - `published`
  - `archived`
- Validate again on the server even when the client already validated.

# XSS

- Sanitize rich-text content before rendering.
- Do not render untrusted HTML directly.
- Do not use `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` without strict sanitization.
- Remove scripts, inline events, unsafe iframes and dangerous URLs.

# File Uploads

- Validate uploads on the server.
- Check the actual file type, not only the extension.
- Allow JPEG, PNG and WebP only unless approved otherwise.
- Generate random filenames.
- Prevent path traversal.
- Re-encode and compress images before storage.
- Remove unnecessary metadata.
- Reject corrupted or unsafe files.
- Do not save the oversized original image.

# Secrets

- Store secrets only in environment variables.
- Never commit `.env` files.
- Never expose secrets through `NEXT_PUBLIC_`.
- Never log secrets, passwords, tokens or API keys.
- Never paste secrets into AI tools.

# Errors

- Do not expose SQL errors, stack traces, table names or internal paths.
- Return generic Hebrew error messages.
- Log only the minimum technical information required.

# RLS

- Enable RLS on every table.
- Anonymous users may read only published public content.
- Only the authenticated administrator may manage content.
- Contact messages may be read only by the administrator.
- Audit logs may not be edited through the browser.